{# A list rather than a table: `.article__body` styles headings, paragraphs and
   lists, and tables are styled only under `.readme`, which this page does not
   carry. Each entry is the cookie's real name, so a reader can match it against
   what their browser shows them.

   The vendors' own cookies are described but not named. This app does not set
   them and does not control what they are called, and a list copied from a
   vendor's documentation would be a list this codebase cannot keep true. #}
<h2>What this site sets</h2>
<p>
    Three cookies, all first-party, none of them shared with anybody:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>PHPSESSID</strong> &mdash; the session. It is what holds a
        booking together across the checkout pages, and what
        <a href="/my/bookings">My bookings</a> recognises you by. It carries no
        data itself, only an identifier. Gone when you close the browser.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>tb_recent_searches</strong> &mdash; the last few searches you
        ran, so the search form can offer them back. It holds the search
        addresses and nothing else. Lasts a year.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>tb_saved_flights</strong> &mdash; the flights you starred, which
        is what <a href="/my/saved">saved flights</a> lists. Written by this
        site&rsquo;s own script in your browser rather than by the server, and
        sent back with every request so the pages can mark what you saved. Lasts
        a year.
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    All three are limited to this site, and the first two cannot be read by
    scripts at all. None of them is used for advertising.
</p>

<h2>What the analytics vendors set</h2>
<p>
    Two scripts, and neither one loads unless you have allowed it. Until you
    answer the notice, nothing below runs and none of these cookies exists:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Google Analytics</strong> &mdash; counts pages and visits.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Yandex Metrika</strong> &mdash; counts the same things, and is
        configured here with <strong>Webvisor</strong> on, which records
        movement, scrolling, clicks and typing on the page and plays them back.
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    Those cookies belong to Google and Yandex, not to this site. What they are
    called, how long they last and what is done with them is set by those
    companies under their own policies.
</p>

<h2>Your answer, and changing it</h2>
<p>
    {% if not consent.answered %}
        You have not answered yet, so no analytics is running.
    {% elseif consent.accepted %}
        You have allowed analytics. Both scripts are loading.
    {% else %}
        You have declined analytics. Neither script is loading.
    {% endif %}
    The answer is kept in a cookie of its own,
    <strong>{{ consent.cookie }}</strong>, and in nothing else &mdash; so
    clearing this site&rsquo;s cookies also clears the answer, and the notice
    comes back.
</p>
<p>
    <button type="button" class="cookie-reset js-cookie-reset"
            data-cookie="{{ consent.cookie }}">Ask me again</button>
</p>

<h2>Turning off the rest</h2>
<p>
    The three above are not covered by that answer, because the site stops
    working without them. Every browser can block or clear cookies for one
    site. Doing that to {{ config('app.name') }} costs you the session &mdash;
    the checkout will not hold together, and My bookings and saved flights will
    be empty &mdash; but the rest works, and search, the place pages and the
    fare calendar need no cookie at all.
</p>
